  "priority": 19,
  "slug": "plugin-11-layer-content-from-a-viewnode-paint-and-input-through-the-layer-stack",
  "title": "plugin-11: Layer content from a ViewNode + paint and input through the layer stack",
  "discussedAt": "",
  "contextReady": false,
  "contextReadyReason": "",
  "summary": "The layer registry ships with native content only. Its own comments say the ViewNode path (plugins) and paint/input wiring land later — this is that work.",
  "description": "Transferred from docs/surface-compositor.md §9 on 2026-07-26, before that file was deleted. §§1–8 of\nit are IMPLEMENTED (hint visibility consumes the stack assembled in `active_hint_targets`); §9 was\nmarked DRAFT and is only half built.\n\n═══ WHAT SHIPPED ═══\n`heca/src/chrome/layers.rs` exists with `LayerBand` (semantic z bands, no magic numbers),\n`LayerKind::{Persistent, OnDemand}`, and `ShowLayer`/`HideLayer`/`ToggleLayer` actions.\n\n═══ WHAT DID NOT — THE FILE SAYS SO ITSELF ═══\nlayers.rs:11 — \"content only; the `ViewNode` content path (plugins) and paint/input wiring land\nlater.\" layers.rs:69 repeats it. There is no `LayerContent` enum: a layer holds a native tree, and\nnothing can hand it a description.\n\n═══ THE TARGET MODEL (from §9, verbatim intent) ═══\n    enum LayerContent {\n        Native(Box<dyn Component>),   // built in Rust (chrome, pane header)\n        View(ViewNode),               // data- or plugin-described; the host realize()s it\n    }\nA `Layer` also carries: id, band, kind, `modal` (captures the context, suppressing everything\nbeneath while active), `visible`, and `parent` — an overlay opened FROM a layer is its CHILD, so it\nsits above it. The occluder is read from the realized tree's laid-out bounds, NEVER hardcoded.\n\n`LayerStack` on `AppState` is the single source of truth for layering: layers ordered by\n(band, insertion) with parent nesting, plus `active: LayerId` — the active context.\n\n═══ WHY IT MATTERS — TWO GAPS, ONE FIX ═══\n1. OVERLAYS SHOW NO KEYHINT. A modal that draws its buttons manually has no child subtree, so\n   `collect_hint_targets` finds nothing to hint. The project rule is KeyHint on every action, so\n   those buttons must be REAL components carrying `.hint_target(...)`.\n2. PLUGINS MUST BE ABLE TO ADD LAYERS — an exposé, a panel, a rich modal — without touching\n   paint, input or hint machinery.\nBoth are the same fix: a layer whose content may be a `ViewNode`, realized by the host, where\nevery actionable node gets a hint target automatically.\n\n═══ WORK ═══\n- Add `LayerContent` with the `View(ViewNode)` arm; realize it through the one bridge\n  (`heca/src/chrome/realize.rs`).\n- Paint layers in stack order; route input to the active or modal layer. Today only hint\n  visibility reads the stack.\n- Keep `add_layer` returning a stable `LayerId` a plugin can hold.\n\n═══ BUILD ON, DO NOT DUPLICATE ═══\n`OverlayHost` is NOT a second stack — it is built ON this one. `open_modal` realizes the ViewNode\nbody plus its action buttons into a native tree, pushes it as a Modal-band layer, and resolves the\nreturned result when a button's intent fires. Its overlays ARE layers here. See\ndocs/chrome-and-ui.md Part II §4.\n\n═══ THE INVARIANTS THAT MUST SURVIVE (§6 of the deleted file) ═══\n- NO hardcoded z or levels anywhere. z is tree position; occluders come from real layout geometry.\n  If you are typing a magic number for a layer or a region, stop.\n- Buttons NEVER declare a layer. A button inherits its layer from the surface it is mounted in.\n  Put it in a pane header and it is in the pane context; put it in a dialog and it is in that\n  dialog's context. That is the whole rule.\n- The active context is moved ONLY by context surfaces (exposé, modal) — never by zoom, scroll or\n  provider selection, so the sidebar stays hintable while a pane is zoomed.\n- Occlusion is decided by draw order plus geometry, not by the coarse level. \"Same level\" is not\n  \"same z\" — two surfaces cannot share a tree position, so there are never ties.\n- Overlays and modals are children of their opener, and exclusive when modal.\n- ONE rule for visibility. If you find yourself special-casing a surface's hints, the surface is\n  mis-modelled — fix the model, do not add a filter.\n\n═══ MIGRATION ORDER (from §9, incremental, no big bang) ═══\n1. ~~Introduce the stack, register the existing surfaces, hints read it~~ — DONE.\n2. Convert the confirm dialog to a layer with hintable buttons → closes the overlay-KeyHint gap.\n   Do it the conformant way: as an `OverlayHost::open_modal` with a ViewNode body and actions,\n   not a bespoke modal.\n3. `ShowLayer` on-demand layers → the first native exposé.\n4. The ViewNode content path → plugins add layers.\n\n═══ VERIFICATION ═══\nLayering, paint order and hint visibility are all visible behaviour. Green tests are not\nverification: build it and have the USER confirm a modal's keycaps appear, and that a layer opened\nfrom another sits above it.",
